WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.380
EVAN ANDERSON: So one
thing right at the start,

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.950
I'm not here to sell you NoOps
or even Google as your SRE.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.260
This is based on my
and other's experience

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.050
running services at Google
and with conversations

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.900
with longtime App
Engine customers.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.992
Great DevOps engineers--
or SREs, if you prefer--

00:00:22.992 --> 00:00:25.200
provide a lot of leverage
for moving your engineering

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.380
organization forward
with continuous delivery

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:34.170
and accelerating your ability
to reliably deliver changes.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:38.066
If you don't have anyone
actively doing the DevOps role,

00:00:38.066 --> 00:00:39.690
your most experienced
developers end up

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.856
getting pulled into the
firefighting effort involved

00:00:41.856 --> 00:00:42.972
in running your system.

00:00:42.972 --> 00:00:44.430
So one way or
another, you're going

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:47.377
to end up doing ops and DevOps.

00:00:47.377 --> 00:00:48.960
Well, you may not
end up doing DevOps.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.850
You may end up just
doing operations.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:55.320
We'll talk a little bit about
the difference in a moment.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.570
So every software system
that has customers

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:01.530
is going to need care and
feeding and maintenance.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.930
And experienced
DevOps practitioners

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:07.410
understand how to do
that more efficiently.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.692
So DevOps are great.

00:01:09.692 --> 00:01:11.400
I'm not saying throw
them out the window.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.990
I'm not saying, don't have them.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.210
I'm saying, let's
make them even better.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:19.930
Common ratio for DevOps to
feature software engineers

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:24.160
is something on the order
of five, maybe 10, to one.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.920
For some reason that I'll
talk about in this talk,

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.900
App Engine covers a lot
of your DevOps basics

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.410
for free, letting you spend
more time in your application.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:36.650
That means your DevOps engineers
can get more stuff done.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:39.280
They can move your
organization faster.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.320
Or if you want,
your DevOps talent

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.260
can also spend some time
improving your application

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:48.190
and writing feature code, rather
than chasing around and putting

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:51.640
out fires and fixing
your infrastructure,

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.680
because your infrastructure
will be beautiful and sparkling.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.590
I've spent nine of the last
12 years on call at Google.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.370
Half of that was in SRE.

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:02.870
The other half was as a
developer, supporting systems

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:05.910
that I had written that
were not yet accepted

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:09.270
by SRE because they were
new and not critical enough.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:12.920
If you went to Ben Treynor's
fireside chat earlier,

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:18.570
he and Ben [INAUDIBLE] talked
about how at Google, SRE teams

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:22.350
come in and focus on the
services that are already

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.570
important and make them better.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:28.950
And it's not a given that
you have an SRE team at all.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.294
That's a little different
than some other organizations.

00:02:31.294 --> 00:02:33.210
And App Engine incorporates
a lot of the stuff

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.690
that I've learned about
running reliable services--

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:37.356
it's one of the things
that attracted me

00:02:37.356 --> 00:02:39.980
to working on App Engine--

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:43.160
for scaling both to millions
of queries per second

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.822
and running hundreds of services
with a single small team.

00:02:47.822 --> 00:02:49.280
Switching to App
Engine isn't going

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.605
to double your productivity.

00:02:50.605 --> 00:02:51.980
I don't think I
could promise you

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:53.360
anything that would do that.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.490
But it can give you a
measurable improvement

00:02:55.490 --> 00:03:00.020
in how much time you spend
on feature development,

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:05.119
rather than on the operation
stuff that has to get done.

00:03:05.119 --> 00:03:07.410
Since we're talking about
spending time on development,

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.080
I'm going to talk first about
why you might be spending

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.340
a lot of time on operations.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:15.730
So Google SRE has a
concept called toil.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:17.920
And toil is work that
you do need to do today.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.295
It'll come back tomorrow, and
you'll have to do it again.

00:03:20.295 --> 00:03:22.360
It's not optional.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.690
You need it, in order to
keep your service running.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.400
Additionally, toil
doesn't necessarily

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.374
require a lot of judgment
or creative problem solving.

00:03:31.374 --> 00:03:35.610
But it has to be done
right, or your system

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:38.940
will be down or broken or slow.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.070
You might recognize
some of these sorts

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:43.740
of things as maintenance
operation procedures

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:47.580
or other best practices document
for handling various issues

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.810
that come up with the service.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:51.810
Now MOPs can be a good
idea for operations

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.210
that happen infrequently,
like a manual database

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.434
failover, if you do that
once or twice a year.

00:03:56.434 --> 00:03:58.350
But if you're using the
same MOP once or twice

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.270
a week, that's probably toil.

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:03.810
And you should be looking at
automating it, rather than just

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.570
streamlining the
work that people do.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:10.680
And the antidote to toil
like this is automation.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.780
So if you can recognize
and understand

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.390
what needs to happen,
you can find ways

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:18.959
to write software that works
around or solves the problem

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:23.790
so that humans can get back
to being creative, thinking,

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:26.700
coding, designing,
listening to customers,

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:29.764
instead of fighting
your infrastructure.

00:04:29.764 --> 00:04:32.180
Now there's a whole lot of
data center automation software

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:33.414
out there already.

00:04:33.414 --> 00:04:35.330
We never talked about
what makes App Engine so

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.114
useful and special.

00:04:37.114 --> 00:04:38.780
I'm going to start
by talking about some

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:43.630
of the other options for data
center automation software.

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:46.350
So at the base of
the stack, we have

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:48.880
what I call fleet
management solutions.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:52.470
These solutions are
targeted managing a fleet--

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:56.861
hundreds, tens, thousands--
of individual heterogeneous

00:04:56.861 --> 00:04:57.360
servers.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.397
They're all
different, or there's

00:04:59.397 --> 00:05:01.980
enough differences between them
that you need to pay attention

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.632
to those differences.

00:05:03.632 --> 00:05:05.340
They may be running
on physical hardware.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:07.006
They may be running
on virtual hardware.

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:08.670
It might be a mix.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:12.540
Oftentimes, you're signing
workloads to individual servers

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.050
statically.

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:16.950
A single server is
for a single workload.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:22.140
And you don't necessarily
automatically scale.

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:24.960
Or if you automatically
scale, it's

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.790
a little bit of
a manual process,

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:32.350
using something like a
managed instance group or--

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:35.050
I forget what the
Amazon equivalent is--

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:38.050
load balanced group.

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:40.410
There is a lot of code
in these fleet management

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.600
tools for rolling out and
managing operating system

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:46.350
and library level patches,
because every machine

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:47.370
is different.

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:48.570
It has its own kernel.

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.640
It has its own
installed libraries.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.000
Maybe some of them are RedHat.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.670
Maybe some of them are Debian.

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:57.330
Maybe you've got some
SunOS there or Windows.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.580
And in addition to
all of that, it also

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.010
rolls out your application
code and manages it.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:05.137
But that's just one of several
jobs that it's got to do.

00:06:05.137 --> 00:06:06.720
Now the great thing
about these things

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:08.340
is they're really adaptable.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:09.810
They're really flexible.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:13.140
And they let you automate
your software deployments

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.474
to your VMs and your
physical machines.

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:16.890
And with the
integration work, you

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.470
can implement health
checking and monitoring

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:22.300
repairs and things like that.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:26.300
So that's actually pretty
good in terms of replacing

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.560
a lot of your toil.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.560
The next level of
abstraction, that you probably

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:32.270
heard about a lot
at this conference,

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:34.740
is container orchestration.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:36.510
So at this layer of
the stack, you're

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:41.100
using something like
Kubernetes or Mesos or Docker.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.160
And you don't worry so much
about managing the base

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:45.370
layer of operating systems.

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:50.510
That's all unified
and pretty abstract.

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:54.549
And instead, you're managing
resource provisioning

00:06:54.549 --> 00:06:56.840
by describing your application
in terms of the resource

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:58.560
requirements it has--

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.660
how much RAM, how much CPU, what
network ports, what storage you

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:04.030
need.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:07.030
And then the orchestration
layer assigns those resources

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:09.160
to physical or virtual
nodes automatically

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:13.530
and can perform failover and
pooling and resource grouping.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:16.030
So resource assignment container
orchestration can be fairly

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:19.870
dynamic and can react to those
failures or the load changes

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:23.124
by rebalancing the cluster,
maybe even moving a workload

00:07:23.124 --> 00:07:25.540
from one machine to another
because you've discovered that

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:30.590
one machine is overloaded, which
is great and introduces a whole

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:34.380
host of new needs that you
never realized you had,

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:37.100
like service discovery-- you
need to keep track of where

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:38.510
everything's running--

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:42.110
and clear abstraction
boundaries between systems,

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:46.070
because you need to actually
deploy and scale them

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:48.070
differently.

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:51.830
So moving up in
the stack this way

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:55.730
frees you from a lot of the OS
patch management and deployment

00:07:55.730 --> 00:07:59.000
toil, because you can describe
things in a high level.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.610
And it can give you resource
efficiency gains by bin

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:04.670
packing multiple containers
onto the same physical node

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:08.610
without managing individual
VMs and operating systems.

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:11.967
Additionally, because resources
are aggregated in this way,

00:08:11.967 --> 00:08:14.300
this can also actually simplify
your hardware build-out,

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:17.180
because you can have
a more uniform fleet

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:20.450
and have the software be
responsible for bin packing

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:22.340
things onto smaller
machines as needed.

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:27.630
And so containers
are a great tool.

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:29.880
And container
orchestration definitely

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:33.390
simplifies packaging
and resource sharing.

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:36.539
But I'm here to talk about
another layer above that that

00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:40.590
removes even more toil and
gives you even more efficiency

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:42.870
benefits and takes
your application

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:44.610
management to the next level.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:48.930
So App Engine is one example
of a platform as a service.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:50.650
Platform as a
service is designed

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:54.220
to understand and cater to a
particular type of application.

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:56.880
So you can see here, we've
got App Engine and Heroku

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:00.000
and Cloud Foundry that are
all in a similar business

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.940
of handling stateless
web services.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:07.260
But things like Lambda
and Cloud Functions

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:09.150
are another platform
as a service that's

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:12.810
targeting a slightly different
market where web services

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:15.030
aren't really their core thing.

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:18.660
It's, run a little piece of
code and spin up and down

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:21.670
very quickly in a
server-less environment.

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:24.390
And then you can
see Dataflow as well

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:28.230
is another platform for
handling your data processing.

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:31.080
You wouldn't use it to
serve a web application.

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:36.570
But it's a great way to
organize your data management

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:40.759
and your ETL pipelines.

00:09:40.759 --> 00:09:42.300
Stateless web
services, which is what

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:44.258
I'm going to focus on
for the rest of the talk,

00:09:44.258 --> 00:09:49.230
covers a lot of ground, from
static or dynamic websites

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:54.390
to APIs and shim APIs,
which can aggregate content

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:56.370
from a number of
different back ends

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:59.790
and provide a seamless
content feed to mobile clients

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:03.020
or to other API consumers.

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:05.430
Stateless web services, while
they cover a lot of ground,

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:07.780
they also have a lot
of things in common.

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:11.110
So that gives us the ability
to provide out-of-the-box

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:14.950
experiences that you'd have to
do a lot of integration on some

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:16.750
of the lower layers to get.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:21.579
So I'll show you an example
in just a moment of what

00:10:21.579 --> 00:10:22.870
the full setup would look like.

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:24.820
But there's a recipe
out there that's

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:28.300
pretty easy to find to
scale an application using

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:29.710
those fleet
management solutions.

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:33.640
It involves Nagios
monitoring, triggering

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:37.390
Chef to do a deployment
based on high CPU usage.

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:39.650
So you find your
Nagios instance.

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:41.230
You put some stuff in there.

00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:44.680
It has to coordinate with
some stuff that's in Chef.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:48.760
And you own all those
integration pieces.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:50.800
App Engine provides
these as scaling knobs

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:52.960
on your application
without needing

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:57.010
for you to explicitly say,
hey, here's my monitoring rule,

00:10:57.010 --> 00:11:00.040
and that means do this,
in terms of deploying

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:01.266
another application node.

00:11:01.266 --> 00:11:03.890
App Engine knows how to do that,
because you're a stateless web

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:06.070
service.

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:08.450
And because your app is
driven by HTTP requests,

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:11.060
we can also give you automatic
latency and request load

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:12.650
monitoring.

00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:14.960
We can also trace your
log statements back

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:18.530
to the particular URL handler
and highlight problem areas

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:20.754
in your application.

00:11:20.754 --> 00:11:22.670
These are some of the
tools that internally we

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:24.950
build in SRE at Google.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:27.800
And by providing this
platform as a service,

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:30.560
we can build them for
you in a scalable way,

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:35.400
as opposed to a bunch of one-off
things for individual services.

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:37.170
So this is an example
of how you could

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:40.587
build your own comparable
platform to some of the stuff

00:11:40.587 --> 00:11:42.420
that I've talked about
that App Engine does.

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:43.980
There's actually
a few more things

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:45.646
that I didn't feel
like putting in here,

00:11:45.646 --> 00:11:48.930
because there's already a lot of
stuff around your application.

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:50.430
So we'd start with
your application.

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:51.920
You start developing.

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:54.630
Let's say you're using Java
and you're using Jetty.

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:56.040
It's great on your desktop.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:57.750
But when you want
to go to production,

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:01.890
you'll need to set up auto
scaling and multiple copies

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:02.784
of your application.

00:12:02.784 --> 00:12:04.200
And then you'll
need to put a load

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:06.480
balancer in front like Nginx.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:08.550
And then to debug
your application,

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.660
you'll set up the ELK stack.

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:14.550
So now you've got Elasticsearch
and Logstash, and Kibana

00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:19.160
that you're running, in addition
to your application in Nginx.

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:21.926
You can still have
Nagios for monitoring.

00:12:21.926 --> 00:12:23.300
So now you've got
some visibility

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:24.710
into what's going on.

00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:28.640
And maybe Nagios is monitoring
your ELK stack, as well as

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:30.500
Nginx in your application.

00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:32.390
And you're getting
all these metrics.

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:34.430
And then you discover
that you're suddenly

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:36.050
running 30 machines.

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:39.530
And so why don't we
go and take SaltStack

00:12:39.530 --> 00:12:41.600
and use that to actually
organize and manage

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:42.719
your roll outs?

00:12:42.719 --> 00:12:43.385
Congratulations.

00:12:43.385 --> 00:12:44.555
Now you've got 31 machines.

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:51.250
And furthermore, I've given
you one set of examples here.

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:53.440
Why don't you replace
Nginx with Envoy?

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:56.660
Or there's a bunch of
different log solutions.

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:59.140
So when you put all the
combinations together,

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:02.200
there's probably 100 different
ways to build this same system.

00:13:04.307 --> 00:13:06.640
Is everyone in your organization
making the same choices

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:07.690
for these?

00:13:07.690 --> 00:13:10.360
Are you building ELK
over here, and you're

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:11.680
using Splunk over there?

00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:16.120
Standardizing on
one set of solutions

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:18.130
makes it a lot easier
for people to move

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:20.080
from one part of the
organization to another

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:22.570
and understand what
tools are available.

00:13:22.570 --> 00:13:26.320
Platform as a service is
one way of standardizing

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:29.740
that set of tools so that
everyone has a common baseline

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:31.320
that they're working from.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:34.060
And they don't need to
learn a new stack of systems

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:36.840
when they go to switch projects.

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:39.780
So assuming that you're buying
what I've got to say so far

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:43.560
and that you're interested in
how App Engine fits into this,

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:46.110
I'm going to explain a little
bit about how App Engine is

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:49.650
organized before I dive
into some of the recipes

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:52.166
and features.

00:13:52.166 --> 00:13:53.540
You don't need to
know this stuff

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:55.212
to start using App Engine.

00:13:55.212 --> 00:13:57.170
And I've met people who've
spent years building

00:13:57.170 --> 00:13:59.060
monolithic applications--

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:02.540
perfectly happy with App
Engine and don't necessarily

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:05.150
understand or worry
about all of this stuff.

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:08.720
But if you're looking to
grow and organize a team

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:11.430
and scale out your
team more efficiently,

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:14.930
it can be useful to understand
how applications and services

00:14:14.930 --> 00:14:16.770
and versions all fit together.

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:19.640
So App Engine subdivides
each project--

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:22.636
which in App Engine, we
call it the top level

00:14:22.636 --> 00:14:24.180
of an application--

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:27.620
into independent services
which can be pushed and scaled

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:28.790
separately.

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:31.850
So you can think of
services like microservices

00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:33.420
that each do a separate thing.

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:37.760
Your microservices can be very
small and have a single URL.

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:40.370
Your microservices
can be not so micro

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:44.330
and can be your entire
application if you want.

00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:46.010
Each service, in
turn, is made up

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:48.360
of a set of deployed versions.

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:50.420
So you do a deployment.

00:14:50.420 --> 00:14:52.160
And that creates a new version.

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:54.260
And at the service
level, you can

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:57.142
choose to shift traffic forward
and backwards between versions.

00:14:57.142 --> 00:14:58.850
So one of the things
that you're deciding

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:05.510
when you split your
application up into services

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:07.340
is, which things should
be pushed together?

00:15:07.340 --> 00:15:10.030
Which things should
be pushed separately?

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:12.120
If you have three API
endpoints that all

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:15.120
touch the same database and
need to use the same schema,

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:16.920
maybe that's all in one service.

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:18.300
If you have another
endpoint that

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:21.464
only talks to videos
and cloud storage,

00:15:21.464 --> 00:15:23.130
maybe that should be
a separate service.

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:25.650
Maybe you'd rather have
it all be bundled together

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:29.920
because that's easier for you
to manage in your CI system.

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:32.260
That's up to you.

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:34.450
Within each version,
App Engine automatically

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:38.560
creates and destroys instances
of each version in response

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:39.340
to load.

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:41.650
So every time you write
code on App Engine,

00:15:41.650 --> 00:15:43.870
you're writing code
for horizontal scaling

00:15:43.870 --> 00:15:45.290
automatically.

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:48.790
And we can expand one version
to be running on thousands

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:54.160
of nodes or scale it down
to zero on standard or one

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:57.730
on the flexible
environment if it's not

00:15:57.730 --> 00:15:58.720
receiving much traffic.

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:04.870
So moving on from the
high level theory,

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:08.620
I'm going to show you some
examples of how App Engine lets

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:11.230
you run your services
faster and smoother

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:14.170
and apply less DevOps effort
to keeping everything running.

00:16:16.649 --> 00:16:18.190
Some of you may have
seen this before

00:16:18.190 --> 00:16:21.850
if you're into SRE-type topics.

00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:26.180
This is Dickerson's
hierarchy of reliability.

00:16:26.180 --> 00:16:28.810
Reliability is one of the
main goals of DevOps--

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:31.900
reliability of delivery,
reliability of the service,

00:16:31.900 --> 00:16:35.290
in terms of uptime, reliability
of process in making sure

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:37.830
that things are repeatable.

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:40.680
And this hierarchy was
proposed by Mikey Dickerson who

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:43.210
was the architect of the
healthcare.gov rescue

00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:45.185
and a former Google SRE.

00:16:45.185 --> 00:16:47.540
I actually worked
with Mikey in Ads SRE.

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:50.340
And this formulation,
based on his experience,

00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:52.300
makes a lot of sense to me.

00:16:52.300 --> 00:16:54.370
And it's based on Maslow's
Hierarchy of Needs

00:16:54.370 --> 00:16:58.492
if you're a psychology person.

00:16:58.492 --> 00:17:00.450
If you haven't seen his
talk on healthcare.gov,

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:03.150
there are several
versions on YouTube.

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:05.280
And it's really
interesting to hear

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:10.020
how he was able to go
into a very broken system

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:13.980
and find ways to bring
that up the hierarchy

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:18.540
and introduce stability
and reliability

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:23.010
into the whole process
and integrate SRE story.

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:26.450
App Engine can help reduce
the amount of effort

00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:28.307
for getting all of these things.

00:17:28.307 --> 00:17:29.890
But I'm going to
particularly focus on

00:17:29.890 --> 00:17:31.420
the ones I've
highlighted in blue.

00:17:31.420 --> 00:17:33.640
I realize that's a lot of them.

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:36.490
But I thought that
more in-depth would

00:17:36.490 --> 00:17:40.660
be a little better than covering
postmortems and development.

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:42.310
So for each of these
layers, I'm going

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:44.050
to be showing you both
the built-in functionality

00:17:44.050 --> 00:17:45.508
and some additional
things that you

00:17:45.508 --> 00:17:47.890
can do to take them
to the next level

00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:51.580
and be more efficient than just
the tools that we ship you.

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:59.180
So starting with monitoring,
I mentioned earlier App Engine

00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:00.650
is a platform as a service.

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:04.010
So we already have top level
metrics on your application.

00:18:04.010 --> 00:18:08.630
Request latency, requests
per second, queue times,

00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:11.180
number of instances, and
all of that sort of thing

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:13.430
can be tracked both across
the application as a whole,

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:16.700
and we can actually also track
things like latency and request

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:19.130
times on specific URLs
if you're interested.

00:18:19.130 --> 00:18:20.630
And the best part
is, you don't need

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:24.050
to add explicit
instrumentation into your code.

00:18:24.050 --> 00:18:26.430
Since we are offering
you the load balancer,

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:27.980
we can integrate
all that monitoring

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:31.160
into the load balancing layer.

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:33.440
And we integrate that with
Stackdriver monitoring

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:34.520
and inspection.

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:38.900
So you can also export specific
application metrics directly

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:42.500
from your code and align them
with the same metrics that are

00:18:42.500 --> 00:18:44.491
coming from your load balancer.

00:18:44.491 --> 00:18:46.490
If you don't want a direct
monitoring dependency

00:18:46.490 --> 00:18:47.540
in your code--

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:51.410
you're worried about either
the latency or the stability--

00:18:51.410 --> 00:18:53.870
you can configure Stackdriver
to export custom metrics

00:18:53.870 --> 00:18:55.010
from your logs.

00:18:55.010 --> 00:18:58.070
And those can be aligned with
an automatically collected

00:18:58.070 --> 00:18:59.270
instance data.

00:18:59.270 --> 00:19:02.060
This allows you to
create a single timeline

00:19:02.060 --> 00:19:04.520
with business metrics like
conversion rate and code

00:19:04.520 --> 00:19:07.360
changes such as
latency improvements.

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:10.165
You can then use the
Stackdriver APIs to aggregate

00:19:10.165 --> 00:19:13.390
and export the data and
run a regression analysis

00:19:13.390 --> 00:19:15.970
to figure out things like
the effect of latency

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:17.470
on conversion rates.

00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:20.050
So putting all your
data in one place

00:19:20.050 --> 00:19:22.690
and giving you powerful
APIs to extract it

00:19:22.690 --> 00:19:26.380
lets you pull out
more value than you

00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:28.650
might if you were putting
everything separately

00:19:28.650 --> 00:19:31.210
and you had some things
in Nagios and some things

00:19:31.210 --> 00:19:34.420
later on in your ETL pipeline.

00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:37.440
Speaking of things
like ETL pipelines,

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:40.480
the stature of a logging API
is really handy for digging

00:19:40.480 --> 00:19:42.490
into issues.

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:44.770
The log viewer is good
for getting a quick view

00:19:44.770 --> 00:19:46.030
into what's going on.

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:49.390
But exporting logs into
BigQuery is amazing.

00:19:49.390 --> 00:19:53.290
How many of you have
used BigQuery before?

00:19:53.290 --> 00:19:56.620
You might know what
I'm talking about.

00:19:56.620 --> 00:19:59.530
So I can use SQL
queries like this

00:19:59.530 --> 00:20:03.940
to dig in and associate error
messages with specific URLs

00:20:03.940 --> 00:20:06.220
for a specific
customer, even if I'm

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:09.310
getting hundreds or thousands
of requests per second

00:20:09.310 --> 00:20:12.340
and I have half a
million customers.

00:20:12.340 --> 00:20:15.430
I had this one customer
issue and they say,

00:20:15.430 --> 00:20:18.520
this stuff all seems to
be wonky, but replies

00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:19.780
are particularly bad.

00:20:19.780 --> 00:20:22.344
So I can dig in and
find out, that's true,

00:20:22.344 --> 00:20:24.010
but they're not doing
very many replies.

00:20:24.010 --> 00:20:27.580
I can also potentially dig
in and get the stack traces.

00:20:27.580 --> 00:20:31.660
I can get any other info
or warning error messages.

00:20:31.660 --> 00:20:35.760
And you can really
pull out a lot of data

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:40.050
by putting things
in BigQuery and then

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:45.330
using SQL to extract out the
information you really need.

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:47.970
As an additional
monitoring tool,

00:20:47.970 --> 00:20:50.410
Stackdriver Trace is pretty
cool for investigating

00:20:50.410 --> 00:20:54.070
the calls made by a particular
request or your back end

00:20:54.070 --> 00:20:55.270
connections.

00:20:55.270 --> 00:20:57.420
And it's automatically
enabled on Standard,

00:20:57.420 --> 00:21:00.820
so you can just try it out
and see what it tells you.

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:02.980
And on Flex or
on-premise, there's

00:21:02.980 --> 00:21:05.620
a library that you can
link in that will give you

00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:07.120
the same information.

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:10.360
And I believe it also integrates
with some open source tracing

00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:11.590
tools.

00:21:11.590 --> 00:21:14.120
I don't remember that name.

00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:15.950
So incident
response-- everyone's

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:18.890
favorite thing to do.

00:21:18.890 --> 00:21:20.540
But when things go
wrong, you really

00:21:20.540 --> 00:21:22.750
want to get things
back working ASAP.

00:21:22.750 --> 00:21:25.790
You've got that visceral "my
customers are angry at me"

00:21:25.790 --> 00:21:27.530
feeling.

00:21:27.530 --> 00:21:31.040
As an SRE, I had to write a lot
of postmortems that basically

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:33.484
concluded, rollback would
have fixed this hours earlier

00:21:33.484 --> 00:21:34.400
than pushing new code.

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:38.680
So by now, my first instinct
whenever I get a page--

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:43.370
and I am on call, not right
now, but for the App Engine

00:21:43.370 --> 00:21:48.170
Management API, I am on
that on call rotation--

00:21:48.170 --> 00:21:50.510
my first instinct is
to find what changed

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:54.290
and roll it back, even if I
don't see any correlation,

00:21:54.290 --> 00:21:57.800
and see if things get better
while I go and dig in further.

00:22:00.610 --> 00:22:03.730
So App Engine, because we
have those multiple deployed

00:22:03.730 --> 00:22:06.100
versions, gives you a
quick way to roll back

00:22:06.100 --> 00:22:07.570
to earlier versions.

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:09.040
It's a single action.

00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:12.190
It takes less than
10 seconds to do.

00:22:12.190 --> 00:22:13.934
And there's also
an audit log so you

00:22:13.934 --> 00:22:15.850
can see what's actually
been changed recently,

00:22:15.850 --> 00:22:20.470
which helps you figure out which
thing you should roll back.

00:22:20.470 --> 00:22:22.720
So as I mentioned,
my team actually

00:22:22.720 --> 00:22:24.744
builds the Management APIs.

00:22:24.744 --> 00:22:27.160
And we talked, when we were
building the traffic splitting

00:22:27.160 --> 00:22:31.870
controls, about building an app
that just lets you roll back.

00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:35.050
And you can see that my
UX skills are probably

00:22:35.050 --> 00:22:37.210
not the ones you'd
want building this.

00:22:37.210 --> 00:22:39.550
So fortunately, we have a
Cloud Console mobile app

00:22:39.550 --> 00:22:41.210
that already lets you do this.

00:22:41.210 --> 00:22:42.820
So don't worry.

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:45.730
You won't have to
suffer through my UI.

00:22:45.730 --> 00:22:50.111
Now obviously, roll back is
not going to fix everything.

00:22:50.111 --> 00:22:52.360
And there are some failures
that App Engine will never

00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:53.620
fix for you.

00:22:53.620 --> 00:22:57.539
But if you look at postmortems
in your organization,

00:22:57.539 --> 00:22:59.830
you'll probably find something
similar to Google, which

00:22:59.830 --> 00:23:04.180
is that code and configuration
pushes are one of the largest

00:23:04.180 --> 00:23:06.490
sources of outages.

00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:07.355
It makes sense.

00:23:07.355 --> 00:23:08.980
You're deliberately
changing something.

00:23:08.980 --> 00:23:11.265
You think you know
what you're doing.

00:23:11.265 --> 00:23:13.390
Well, you knew what you
were doing 90% of the time.

00:23:13.390 --> 00:23:16.180
And the other 10%
doesn't go so well.

00:23:16.180 --> 00:23:19.530
But you tested the
common happy case.

00:23:19.530 --> 00:23:23.220
So roll back solves
those problems.

00:23:23.220 --> 00:23:24.930
It doesn't solve
backend services down.

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:27.740
It doesn't solve
user data corruption.

00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:30.240
Roll back sometimes
will help you there

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:32.280
by changing your
query pattern back

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:36.390
to what it was before or not
corrupting any more user data.

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:39.330
But you'll still have
to fix those yourself.

00:23:39.330 --> 00:23:42.210
And it's still up to you
to make the decisions

00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:47.157
as to when to roll back and how
to use the tools to fix things.

00:23:47.157 --> 00:23:48.990
And if you're using App
Engine consistently,

00:23:48.990 --> 00:23:53.124
the other benefit is that
all your tools look the same.

00:23:53.124 --> 00:23:55.290
And so your most experienced
people from one project

00:23:55.290 --> 00:23:57.510
can help out with
another project

00:23:57.510 --> 00:23:58.890
when everything's on fire.

00:24:02.320 --> 00:24:06.100
So outages-- not so much fun.

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:08.760
Pushing code, seeing
your code go live--

00:24:08.760 --> 00:24:10.090
a lot more fun.

00:24:10.090 --> 00:24:11.890
So test and release--

00:24:11.890 --> 00:24:14.000
much better incident management.

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:15.910
Got to do the incident
response, or you

00:24:15.910 --> 00:24:17.410
don't have any customers.

00:24:17.410 --> 00:24:24.040
But App Engine also has a lot of
tools for testing and releases.

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:27.730
And the multiple versions
is the start of that,

00:24:27.730 --> 00:24:29.600
but not the end of the story.

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:31.930
So you can see here in the
production environment,

00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:34.810
we're doing a traffic shift
from our current release

00:24:34.810 --> 00:24:35.740
to the next release.

00:24:35.740 --> 00:24:37.780
And we're canarying it
with 10% of traffic.

00:24:37.780 --> 00:24:39.280
So we've got some
confidence that it

00:24:39.280 --> 00:24:40.761
doesn't break everything.

00:24:40.761 --> 00:24:42.760
But we're not putting all
our users at risk yet.

00:24:42.760 --> 00:24:46.090
So if something goes
bad for 1% of our users

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:47.480
on the new version,
it's actually

00:24:47.480 --> 00:24:50.630
a 0.1% outage at that moment.

00:24:50.630 --> 00:24:52.720
So I've still got three nines.

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:55.660
It's not too bad.

00:24:55.660 --> 00:24:57.854
Additionally, you
can see that we

00:24:57.854 --> 00:24:59.770
have a test environment
that has a whole bunch

00:24:59.770 --> 00:25:01.150
of other versions running.

00:25:01.150 --> 00:25:04.840
So everybody can have their own
version for development, which,

00:25:04.840 --> 00:25:07.120
my experience has been,
drastically accelerates

00:25:07.120 --> 00:25:12.760
things versus hot seating on
one pre-production environment.

00:25:12.760 --> 00:25:15.814
So what does App Engine not
cover for testing and release?

00:25:15.814 --> 00:25:17.730
Well, you'll still need
to run your own tests.

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:19.390
You'll need to
write them yourself.

00:25:19.390 --> 00:25:23.290
And you still need
your own CI system.

00:25:23.290 --> 00:25:26.044
And you need to choose
your source release

00:25:26.044 --> 00:25:27.460
and start the
deployment yourself.

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:33.520
App Engine has integrations
with tools like Jenkins, Travis,

00:25:33.520 --> 00:25:35.022
and CircleCI.

00:25:35.022 --> 00:25:36.730
There's a lot of other
systems out there.

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:39.460
A lot of them do have
App Engine integrations.

00:25:39.460 --> 00:25:43.180
And I've actually built
my own little toy CI

00:25:43.180 --> 00:25:45.010
system at one point,
just to play around

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:46.150
with the management API.

00:25:46.150 --> 00:25:48.490
And most of my time
was spent figuring out

00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:51.520
how to package up my code,
upload it to Cloud Storage,

00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:55.880
and then the actual API call
at the end is pretty simple.

00:25:55.880 --> 00:25:58.810
So if you're thinking
about building your own,

00:25:58.810 --> 00:26:00.055
it's not too scary.

00:26:00.055 --> 00:26:01.430
There's some work
you need to do.

00:26:01.430 --> 00:26:03.280
But it's not too scary.

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:06.001
It's also easy to
deploy the same code--

00:26:06.001 --> 00:26:07.750
you can see here-- to
the test environment

00:26:07.750 --> 00:26:11.710
as to production with a slightly
different configuration.

00:26:11.710 --> 00:26:15.190
And I've seen a couple different
patterns for doing this.

00:26:15.190 --> 00:26:17.020
One common one is to
have a set of settings

00:26:17.020 --> 00:26:22.840
in Data Store or Cloud Storage
or potentially even Cloud SQL

00:26:22.840 --> 00:26:26.464
where you connect to an address
that's based on your instance

00:26:26.464 --> 00:26:27.880
name or your app
name or something

00:26:27.880 --> 00:26:31.030
like that, which tells you
where to find everything else.

00:26:31.030 --> 00:26:33.820
The other pattern,
which I slightly prefer,

00:26:33.820 --> 00:26:36.940
is to actually define your
configuration settings in code

00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:41.410
and use a configuration
class which you select

00:26:41.410 --> 00:26:43.120
which one of these to use.

00:26:43.120 --> 00:26:45.550
And the benefit of that is
that all your configuration

00:26:45.550 --> 00:26:47.530
changes end up in source
control automatically.

00:26:47.530 --> 00:26:50.100
You don't wonder, hey, who
changed this data store

00:26:50.100 --> 00:26:55.445
setting, or how did this config
file get uploaded to GCS?

00:26:55.445 --> 00:26:57.695
So I'm going to show you an
example of how that works.

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:04.050
So you can see here, I defined
the base configuration class.

00:27:04.050 --> 00:27:06.360
That could be development
environment settings.

00:27:06.360 --> 00:27:07.275
That could just be--

00:27:09.810 --> 00:27:10.970
in this case, it's none.

00:27:10.970 --> 00:27:13.657
If you're using a base config,
you're probably doing it wrong.

00:27:13.657 --> 00:27:15.490
You should be getting
one of the other ones.

00:27:15.490 --> 00:27:18.749
And it makes it more obvious
when something goes wrong.

00:27:18.749 --> 00:27:21.040
Then you can say, I defined
a production configuration.

00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:25.870
And then this magic
config basically

00:27:25.870 --> 00:27:28.450
chooses one of the
other configuration

00:27:28.450 --> 00:27:32.230
classes and extracts
values from it if there

00:27:32.230 --> 00:27:35.260
isn't an overriding
environment variable setting.

00:27:35.260 --> 00:27:38.560
And then you can see
my top level config

00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:42.760
object is an eval that
picks one of these classes

00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:47.447
and defaults to development
so that if someone forgets

00:27:47.447 --> 00:27:49.280
to actually define the
environment variable,

00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:52.640
they don't start running
against production.

00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:54.259
Referencing this
in source control,

00:27:54.259 --> 00:27:56.300
you can get code reviews
up when you're doing it.

00:27:56.300 --> 00:27:58.940
You can actually
test your values

00:27:58.940 --> 00:28:01.730
and make sure that you don't
end up with conflicting values

00:28:01.730 --> 00:28:05.380
as your configuration
gets more complex.

00:28:05.380 --> 00:28:07.704
And also, when you can
make configuration changes,

00:28:07.704 --> 00:28:10.120
you can roll them out with
traffic splitting, the same way

00:28:10.120 --> 00:28:11.619
that you roll out
your code changes.

00:28:11.619 --> 00:28:15.190
Adding a new backend,
maybe you typo-ed it wrong.

00:28:15.190 --> 00:28:20.190
It's good to have that be a slow
process rather than a Big Bang

00:28:20.190 --> 00:28:22.980
switch over.

00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:27.570
So far, we talked about the
basics of app development.

00:28:27.570 --> 00:28:31.464
But not that we've talked about
keep your head above water,

00:28:31.464 --> 00:28:33.130
capacity planning is
one of those things

00:28:33.130 --> 00:28:35.440
that you always want to
get to in the future.

00:28:35.440 --> 00:28:39.070
But App Engine actually
lets you get to it never.

00:28:39.070 --> 00:28:43.294
And that's OK, because App
Engine has automatic scaling.

00:28:43.294 --> 00:28:44.710
It will scale down
to zero, and it

00:28:44.710 --> 00:28:49.660
will scale out to thousands
of instances automatically.

00:28:49.660 --> 00:28:52.060
And you don't need to
do capacity planning

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:54.550
and reservations
and decide, am I

00:28:54.550 --> 00:28:56.980
going to need this capacity
for one year or three years,

00:28:56.980 --> 00:29:01.480
or even do I need to
request a quota increase?

00:29:01.480 --> 00:29:03.040
App Engine can
automatically scale

00:29:03.040 --> 00:29:10.000
for spiky events with low
latency past 20,000 QPS.

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:14.280
And for small spikes--

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:17.680
of course, small is
somewhat relative--

00:29:17.680 --> 00:29:19.709
you don't even need
to contact Google.

00:29:19.709 --> 00:29:22.000
If you're thinking about
launching the next Pokemon Go,

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:23.041
we might like a heads-up.

00:29:25.217 --> 00:29:26.800
But if you're not
launching Pokemon Go

00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:29.410
and you're launching something
that's just going a little bit

00:29:29.410 --> 00:29:35.462
viral, then shouldn't need
to talk to Google at all.

00:29:35.462 --> 00:29:36.920
So as I mentioned,
the Standard run

00:29:36.920 --> 00:29:39.350
time, support scale
to zero, which

00:29:39.350 --> 00:29:41.960
makes standard runtimes great
for keeping around development

00:29:41.960 --> 00:29:46.460
tools and dashboards and stuff
that's not used all the time.

00:29:46.460 --> 00:29:48.800
But every so often, you want
to be able to pull it out

00:29:48.800 --> 00:29:51.140
and you want it to be
running immediately.

00:29:51.140 --> 00:29:54.260
Standard is also much faster
for deployment than Flex.

00:29:54.260 --> 00:29:57.710
So if you're trying to iterate
on your development cycle,

00:29:57.710 --> 00:30:01.760
you may find that deploying
to Standard is faster.

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:03.950
The Flex environment
requires a VM

00:30:03.950 --> 00:30:06.680
to be running in
order to collect

00:30:06.680 --> 00:30:09.020
all of the metrics
needed for scaling

00:30:09.020 --> 00:30:17.905
and to determine how much
load and so forth is going on.

00:30:17.905 --> 00:30:20.030
But you can stop the versions
that you're not using

00:30:20.030 --> 00:30:21.420
and stop getting
billed for them.

00:30:21.420 --> 00:30:25.060
So we have a lot
of customers who

00:30:25.060 --> 00:30:28.510
are using App Engine with
things like Datastore

00:30:28.510 --> 00:30:33.550
or a Bigtable who scale out very
wide on their App Engine side.

00:30:33.550 --> 00:30:36.527
And if you're using
something like Cloud SQL,

00:30:36.527 --> 00:30:38.610
you'll have to provision
your Cloud SQL instances.

00:30:38.610 --> 00:30:42.050
So if you can use a
horizontally scalable system

00:30:42.050 --> 00:30:44.570
for your storage, you might
not have to do any capacity

00:30:44.570 --> 00:30:47.750
planning at all.

00:30:47.750 --> 00:30:50.300
Still have to do
budget planning.

00:30:50.300 --> 00:30:51.570
I'd run some demos for you.

00:30:51.570 --> 00:30:54.730
I wish I had done budget
planning beforehand.

00:30:54.730 --> 00:30:56.890
So Cloud Console,
which you can see here,

00:30:56.890 --> 00:30:59.514
and the Stackdriver UI,
which I'll show you later,

00:30:59.514 --> 00:31:01.180
cover a lot of the
basic user experience

00:31:01.180 --> 00:31:03.190
for managing your app.

00:31:03.190 --> 00:31:05.680
And another thing to
note is the Cloud Console

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:07.780
is building on
Google's public APIs.

00:31:07.780 --> 00:31:11.920
So you can also extract all
this data to your own consoles

00:31:11.920 --> 00:31:15.140
if you're building your
own view of the world.

00:31:15.140 --> 00:31:16.612
So before I talk
in more detail, I

00:31:16.612 --> 00:31:18.820
wanted to highlight a couple
of the cool things we've

00:31:18.820 --> 00:31:20.110
got built in.

00:31:20.110 --> 00:31:23.770
On the left hand side, you
can see the Cloud Trace

00:31:23.770 --> 00:31:25.990
suggestions.

00:31:25.990 --> 00:31:29.379
It's automatically identified
several issues with Datastore.

00:31:29.379 --> 00:31:30.670
You probably can't see it here.

00:31:30.670 --> 00:31:34.580
But it says, use of offset
and data store queries,

00:31:34.580 --> 00:31:37.072
which is an anti-pattern,
because when you use an offset,

00:31:37.072 --> 00:31:39.280
Datastore still has to read
through the first things.

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:41.680
It just throws them way to
hand you the next stuff.

00:31:41.680 --> 00:31:43.600
Use query cursors instead.

00:31:43.600 --> 00:31:47.050
Many successive data
store put and get calls.

00:31:47.050 --> 00:31:50.020
So by doing these
separately, my app

00:31:50.020 --> 00:31:52.570
is paying a latency
penalty each time

00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:55.660
I do a put or a get, whereas if
I did multiple at the same time

00:31:55.660 --> 00:31:59.290
or parallelized
them, I would have

00:31:59.290 --> 00:32:02.080
much lower latency overall.

00:32:02.080 --> 00:32:03.840
Because we understand
your architecture

00:32:03.840 --> 00:32:09.960
and how your backend RPCs relate
to your frontend URL requests,

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:14.940
we can actually determine that
things like the consecutive put

00:32:14.940 --> 00:32:17.580
and get calls are coming from
the same handler, as opposed

00:32:17.580 --> 00:32:22.610
to different handlers that
happen to have similar timing.

00:32:22.610 --> 00:32:24.822
And so we can suggest these
sorts of improvements.

00:32:24.822 --> 00:32:26.280
On the right hand
side, you can see

00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:31.080
the error reporting has picked
up a bunch of 500's that we've

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:34.050
served to customers
over the last day

00:32:34.050 --> 00:32:36.900
and highlighted particular
issues that we should probably

00:32:36.900 --> 00:32:39.920
be looking at and fixing.

00:32:39.920 --> 00:32:42.770
Again, this is stuff that
we've automatically built in.

00:32:42.770 --> 00:32:48.077
So you could obviously run
this stuff yourself and go

00:32:48.077 --> 00:32:49.160
through the logs yourself.

00:32:49.160 --> 00:32:51.590
But we've already given
you a basic version that

00:32:51.590 --> 00:32:53.870
will often meet your needs.

00:32:53.870 --> 00:32:55.370
But sometimes you
know your workflow

00:32:55.370 --> 00:32:57.000
better than what we do.

00:32:57.000 --> 00:32:59.930
And so I'm going to talk
a little bit about how

00:32:59.930 --> 00:33:01.910
you can make an even
better UX for you

00:33:01.910 --> 00:33:04.480
for your team that maybe
isn't the same as the UX

00:33:04.480 --> 00:33:07.410
that the next would build.

00:33:07.410 --> 00:33:11.054
So with all these APIs,
there's lots of great ways

00:33:11.054 --> 00:33:13.470
that you can integrate them
together-- some of them mostly

00:33:13.470 --> 00:33:16.320
using Cloud Platform
technologies, some

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:20.160
of them using just
a few, but using

00:33:20.160 --> 00:33:25.994
some common patterns to make
it easier to debug production.

00:33:25.994 --> 00:33:27.410
And the other thing
is, of course,

00:33:27.410 --> 00:33:29.140
if you find these
useful, do them.

00:33:29.140 --> 00:33:32.380
If you don't-- if that sounds
like a terrible idea to you--

00:33:32.380 --> 00:33:35.260
there's nothing that says
everyone must do things

00:33:35.260 --> 00:33:37.599
the same way in production.

00:33:37.599 --> 00:33:38.890
Your team should be consistent.

00:33:38.890 --> 00:33:41.740
But you don't have to be
consistent with the team

00:33:41.740 --> 00:33:44.060
in another company.

00:33:44.060 --> 00:33:47.334
So for us, we have
a mailing list

00:33:47.334 --> 00:33:49.000
for recent push
announcements and status

00:33:49.000 --> 00:33:51.700
updates about production.

00:33:51.700 --> 00:33:53.080
In our push
instructions, we used

00:33:53.080 --> 00:33:55.190
to have a line that said,
send mail to the mailing

00:33:55.190 --> 00:33:56.950
list about the push.

00:33:56.950 --> 00:33:58.282
There's two problems with this.

00:33:58.282 --> 00:34:00.490
The first one is that the
instruction didn't actually

00:34:00.490 --> 00:34:01.780
tell you what to say.

00:34:01.780 --> 00:34:04.600
So every person had to
interpret that individually.

00:34:04.600 --> 00:34:08.710
And the second one was that
a person had to do this.

00:34:08.710 --> 00:34:11.650
And it wasn't just a robot
automatically sending

00:34:11.650 --> 00:34:17.230
something like a summary or
hey, this push has started.

00:34:17.230 --> 00:34:20.199
So a lot of you probably use
Slack or IRC rather than email

00:34:20.199 --> 00:34:23.710
for keeping track of what's
going on in production.

00:34:23.710 --> 00:34:25.857
You can automatically
update Slack

00:34:25.857 --> 00:34:27.940
when there are production
changes, such as traffic

00:34:27.940 --> 00:34:30.940
splits, version deployments,
or permission changes,

00:34:30.940 --> 00:34:34.360
by setting up a logs
filter on the audit log

00:34:34.360 --> 00:34:40.120
and using that to publish just
those audit logs to Pub/Sub.

00:34:40.120 --> 00:34:44.770
So you can say, just
users added to a ACL,

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:48.610
or just traffic splits,
or new versions in traffic

00:34:48.610 --> 00:34:52.480
splits but don't tell me
about deleting versions.

00:34:52.480 --> 00:34:56.870
And Cloud Functions can pull
things off of that queue

00:34:56.870 --> 00:34:59.360
and automatically update
a Slack topic telling you

00:34:59.360 --> 00:35:02.090
everything that's going on in
production that you care about.

00:35:02.090 --> 00:35:04.040
And then all the rest
of the stuff, you

00:35:04.040 --> 00:35:06.020
don't have to go and
look at the audit log.

00:35:06.020 --> 00:35:07.853
It's already in the
tool that you're already

00:35:07.853 --> 00:35:10.910
looking at every day.

00:35:10.910 --> 00:35:16.460
You probably also track which
commits fix a particular issue.

00:35:16.460 --> 00:35:19.549
But if you've run production
systems for a while,

00:35:19.549 --> 00:35:21.590
there's a difference
between the fix is committed

00:35:21.590 --> 00:35:26.160
to source control and the fix is
actually running in production.

00:35:26.160 --> 00:35:28.620
It's a very important
distinction.

00:35:28.620 --> 00:35:31.520
And sometimes when
you have to roll back,

00:35:31.520 --> 00:35:34.400
you want to know, what's going
to break when I roll back?

00:35:34.400 --> 00:35:39.590
So if you have a consistent way
of tracking which CLs are going

00:35:39.590 --> 00:35:42.260
into a particular release
and naming your releases

00:35:42.260 --> 00:35:46.340
in a way that's
coherent, you can

00:35:46.340 --> 00:35:48.180
update your Jira
or whatever you're

00:35:48.180 --> 00:35:50.870
using for issue tracking
to indicate which

00:35:50.870 --> 00:35:52.550
things will be broken
when you roll back

00:35:52.550 --> 00:35:55.310
to a particular version.

00:35:55.310 --> 00:35:58.300
And so roll back is great.

00:35:58.300 --> 00:36:00.582
But sometimes there
is a bad thing that

00:36:00.582 --> 00:36:01.790
will happen at the same time.

00:36:01.790 --> 00:36:03.460
So you need to do some
mitigating steps or something

00:36:03.460 --> 00:36:04.220
like that.

00:36:04.220 --> 00:36:06.550
And this gives you some
operational awareness

00:36:06.550 --> 00:36:08.620
of exactly what's going on.

00:36:08.620 --> 00:36:12.930
And this last integration
is a really cool one

00:36:12.930 --> 00:36:14.830
that I've seen some
teams build at Google.

00:36:14.830 --> 00:36:18.970
But everyone seems to have
to build their own solution.

00:36:18.970 --> 00:36:24.490
And a lot of this is because
what makes a release really bad

00:36:24.490 --> 00:36:26.560
is not universal.

00:36:26.560 --> 00:36:28.840
For some people,
error rate increase.

00:36:28.840 --> 00:36:32.290
For some people, it's latency.

00:36:32.290 --> 00:36:34.840
You may have other metrics.

00:36:34.840 --> 00:36:36.700
But you can have a
Stackdriver alert--

00:36:36.700 --> 00:36:39.460
assuming you can describe
what makes a bad release,

00:36:39.460 --> 00:36:41.770
you can have a
Stackdriver alert that

00:36:41.770 --> 00:36:44.620
detects if there's a problem
like an error rate increase

00:36:44.620 --> 00:36:48.820
and sends a web hook or an SNS
notification to your control

00:36:48.820 --> 00:36:51.550
stack to automatically
roll back that release

00:36:51.550 --> 00:36:54.130
before a human even
has to get involved.

00:36:54.130 --> 00:36:57.080
You may still want to tell a
human that this is happening.

00:36:57.080 --> 00:36:58.979
And you may even
want to page them.

00:36:58.979 --> 00:37:00.520
And if you're building
such a system,

00:37:00.520 --> 00:37:03.340
you probably also want a Dead
Man switch so that you're not

00:37:03.340 --> 00:37:06.010
fighting the automation if
the release needs to go out

00:37:06.010 --> 00:37:07.926
or you really need to
make some other changes.

00:37:11.840 --> 00:37:15.170
So I've talked a
whole bunch of talk

00:37:15.170 --> 00:37:19.580
about ways that you can
make things more efficient.

00:37:19.580 --> 00:37:22.220
Now I'm going to do a demo,
because my understanding is

00:37:22.220 --> 00:37:23.780
that demos are
part of the reason

00:37:23.780 --> 00:37:25.940
that people come and
watch this stuff--

00:37:25.940 --> 00:37:29.400
see whether the demo
burst into flames or not.

00:37:29.400 --> 00:37:32.330
So I'm not to show how you can
take off-the-shelf software not

00:37:32.330 --> 00:37:35.000
designed for App Engine
and run it on App Engine

00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:37.160
and get some of those
App Engine benefits.

00:37:37.160 --> 00:37:41.330
In particular, I'm going to use
Jira, which is a closed source

00:37:41.330 --> 00:37:43.010
issue and project tracker.

00:37:43.010 --> 00:37:46.680
Typically, you run
it on a singleton VM.

00:37:46.680 --> 00:37:48.910
And since it's closed
source, I really--

00:37:48.910 --> 00:37:51.080
oh, don't-- OK, you
can cut over now.

00:37:51.080 --> 00:37:54.110
But I still have a little bit
more to talk about before--

00:37:54.110 --> 00:37:55.360
definitely don't cut over now.

00:37:55.360 --> 00:37:58.440
I actually have a
couple more slides, too.

00:37:58.440 --> 00:38:00.240
So I can't really make
any software changes

00:38:00.240 --> 00:38:02.970
to the Jira software
itself to make

00:38:02.970 --> 00:38:06.200
it run better on App Engine.

00:38:06.200 --> 00:38:09.230
I do use the flex
environment here.

00:38:09.230 --> 00:38:11.480
But I thought that it would
be interesting to show you

00:38:11.480 --> 00:38:14.570
a real application being
put on App Engine that

00:38:14.570 --> 00:38:16.520
wasn't designed that way.

00:38:16.520 --> 00:38:20.720
And Jira seemed like, hey,
this is something that probably

00:38:20.720 --> 00:38:23.460
a lot of you have seen or used.

00:38:23.460 --> 00:38:27.280
So my architecture
looks pretty simple.

00:38:27.280 --> 00:38:29.130
My developers talk to Jira.

00:38:29.130 --> 00:38:30.530
Running it on App Engine.

00:38:30.530 --> 00:38:33.270
I'm using Cloud SQL as
the backend database,

00:38:33.270 --> 00:38:35.740
because I'm doing this
to not managed servers.

00:38:35.740 --> 00:38:38.262
So I'm not going to set up
my own server, my own Oracle

00:38:38.262 --> 00:38:39.720
or Postgres or
something like that.

00:38:39.720 --> 00:38:43.070
I'm going to use
what Google has.

00:38:43.070 --> 00:38:45.556
And I'm using Flex,
because I'm going

00:38:45.556 --> 00:38:47.180
to need to run a
little additional code

00:38:47.180 --> 00:38:49.700
inside the Docker container
that's not the main web

00:38:49.700 --> 00:38:51.010
application process.

00:38:51.010 --> 00:38:56.910
And one of the great things
about the Flex environment,

00:38:56.910 --> 00:39:00.160
if you're trying to do
something like this,

00:39:00.160 --> 00:39:03.910
is that in addition
to Java and Node

00:39:03.910 --> 00:39:06.850
and PHP and all those
other lovely runtimes,

00:39:06.850 --> 00:39:09.460
Flex can run raw
Docker containers.

00:39:09.460 --> 00:39:13.170
So I start with a container
that's built on the Google Java

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:16.240
runtime, and then I
do a bunch of work

00:39:16.240 --> 00:39:20.050
to install Jira into MySQL
connector and so forth.

00:39:20.050 --> 00:39:22.030
I set it to be on
the correct port.

00:39:22.030 --> 00:39:26.500
And then you can see that I
copy in a wrapper script that

00:39:26.500 --> 00:39:31.960
does all the fixes I
need and a core database

00:39:31.960 --> 00:39:35.560
config, although that wrapper
actually takes an environment

00:39:35.560 --> 00:39:38.089
variable and puts it into
the DD config at runtime

00:39:38.089 --> 00:39:40.630
so that I can change which Cloud
SQL instance I'm talking to.

00:39:43.330 --> 00:39:47.500
So one interesting
feature of Jira

00:39:47.500 --> 00:39:51.520
is that it's got this
database for all your issues.

00:39:51.520 --> 00:39:55.240
But it actually also builds
a local on-disk Lecene index

00:39:55.240 --> 00:39:58.210
in order to be able
to search all of them.

00:39:58.210 --> 00:40:00.887
So that's kind of a big
stateful local cache.

00:40:00.887 --> 00:40:02.845
And we're talking about
stateless web services.

00:40:06.100 --> 00:40:08.830
Fortunately, Jira has
a re-indexing API.

00:40:08.830 --> 00:40:13.570
So I can use that at
start up and periodically

00:40:13.570 --> 00:40:16.450
after that, re-index
my database, in order

00:40:16.450 --> 00:40:20.620
to get Jira to actually have
Search working, and do things,

00:40:20.620 --> 00:40:25.750
like which issues are assigned
to me, actually shows up.

00:40:25.750 --> 00:40:29.140
I also use basic scaling
or manual scaling

00:40:29.140 --> 00:40:32.110
to keep exactly one instance
running per version so

00:40:32.110 --> 00:40:34.780
that there's less chance of
updates going to one server

00:40:34.780 --> 00:40:36.970
and then needing to call
re-index on another.

00:40:36.970 --> 00:40:38.860
If you were scaling
out Jira really large,

00:40:38.860 --> 00:40:40.390
that might be a problem.

00:40:40.390 --> 00:40:43.030
Most of the time, if
you're getting five QPS

00:40:43.030 --> 00:40:47.120
to your Jira instance,
something's probably wrong.

00:40:47.120 --> 00:40:48.535
So now if you
could switch over--

00:40:52.600 --> 00:40:55.840
so I'm running this
off of my Chromebook

00:40:55.840 --> 00:40:58.150
since that's my primary
development machine.

00:40:58.150 --> 00:41:01.210
But this means that I
need someplace where

00:41:01.210 --> 00:41:03.130
I can run the raw VM commands.

00:41:03.130 --> 00:41:07.870
So my typical pattern is to spin
up a worker VM in the project

00:41:07.870 --> 00:41:09.130
that I'm using.

00:41:09.130 --> 00:41:11.350
And it makes it really
easy to install tools

00:41:11.350 --> 00:41:13.670
for demos and things like that.

00:41:13.670 --> 00:41:17.140
It also means that I won't
have any fancy pretty editors.

00:41:17.140 --> 00:41:21.514
And instead, I'll be mostly
using tools like VI and less.

00:41:26.260 --> 00:41:27.130
Here we go.

00:41:27.130 --> 00:41:28.620
We have SSH.

00:41:28.620 --> 00:41:30.972
Let's see.

00:41:30.972 --> 00:41:31.930
Can everyone read that?

00:41:37.941 --> 00:41:38.440
OK.

00:41:38.440 --> 00:41:45.300
So before this talk, I actually
built a new Jira image based

00:41:45.300 --> 00:41:50.250
on the fresh out-of-the-oven
latest 732 release from March

00:41:50.250 --> 00:41:51.660
1.

00:41:51.660 --> 00:41:53.430
It was great that they
shipped a release,

00:41:53.430 --> 00:41:57.180
because that meant that
I wasn't just installing

00:41:57.180 --> 00:42:00.030
the same software again, because
I started working on this demo

00:42:00.030 --> 00:42:01.650
long before they shipped it.

00:42:01.650 --> 00:42:06.720
So you can see in the Container
Registry I have a base

00:42:06.720 --> 00:42:09.470
image and a Jira 732 image.

00:42:12.840 --> 00:42:14.570
Now I'm going to go back.

00:42:14.570 --> 00:42:18.630
And here is my command line.

00:42:18.630 --> 00:42:22.145
So I'll tell you a little
bit about what this is doing.

00:42:22.145 --> 00:42:25.260
I don't need help either.

00:42:25.260 --> 00:42:27.570
So I'm deploying a
version named 732

00:42:27.570 --> 00:42:30.720
based on this Jira
image and an app.yaml

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:32.760
that I have in the
same directory.

00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:34.515
The app.yaml looks
pretty simple.

00:42:41.410 --> 00:42:45.600
So I say it's a custom
runtime running on Flex.

00:42:45.600 --> 00:42:47.730
I give it an instance
tag, just in case I

00:42:47.730 --> 00:42:49.100
needed special firewall rules.

00:42:49.100 --> 00:42:50.970
It turns out, I don't.

00:42:50.970 --> 00:42:54.690
I assign two CPUs
and two gigs of RAM.

00:42:54.690 --> 00:42:57.240
I turn of health
checks, because Jira

00:42:57.240 --> 00:43:00.250
doesn't know to answer 200 at
the App Engine health check

00:43:00.250 --> 00:43:01.080
URL.

00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:04.170
So I just say, don't
worry about that.

00:43:04.170 --> 00:43:06.922
And I have Stackdriver
monitoring do that instead.

00:43:06.922 --> 00:43:08.380
And you can see at
the bottom, I've

00:43:08.380 --> 00:43:11.460
set manual scaling
to one instance

00:43:11.460 --> 00:43:14.160
So that I don't end up
with multiple copies,

00:43:14.160 --> 00:43:17.670
each with their own
big, fat, local cache.

00:43:17.670 --> 00:43:22.290
The last two flags say,
whichever version's

00:43:22.290 --> 00:43:24.660
running now, don't stop that.

00:43:24.660 --> 00:43:29.010
The default for Flex, to
save money and confusion,

00:43:29.010 --> 00:43:32.910
is that your old
versions are shut down

00:43:32.910 --> 00:43:35.670
when you deploy a new version
so that you don't end up

00:43:35.670 --> 00:43:39.390
paying for half dozen in
VMs as you're developing.

00:43:39.390 --> 00:43:43.470
The no promote flag says, I'm
deploying this new version,

00:43:43.470 --> 00:43:45.780
but please don't
make it live yet.

00:43:45.780 --> 00:43:48.970
I'll show you later on in
the demo how we make it live.

00:43:48.970 --> 00:43:51.120
Obviously, if you
don't say no promote,

00:43:51.120 --> 00:43:54.990
we could just have it happen
as soon as it's ready.

00:43:54.990 --> 00:43:57.980
So as I mentioned,
the Flex environment

00:43:57.980 --> 00:43:59.720
can take a few minutes
to start the VMs,

00:43:59.720 --> 00:44:01.700
configure the load
balancing, and so forth.

00:44:01.700 --> 00:44:03.110
And Jira also
takes a few minutes

00:44:03.110 --> 00:44:05.210
to start from a fresh install.

00:44:05.210 --> 00:44:08.420
So while that's happening,
I'm going to show you--

00:44:08.420 --> 00:44:11.090
I'm going to say "yes,"
because otherwise, we

00:44:11.090 --> 00:44:14.670
would not get any further.

00:44:14.670 --> 00:44:18.700
And now I am going to show
you what we have running.

00:44:18.700 --> 00:44:20.590
So you can see we've got Jira.

00:44:20.590 --> 00:44:23.748
It looks pretty normal.

00:44:23.748 --> 00:44:26.441
If I log in, you
can see, oh, look,

00:44:26.441 --> 00:44:28.190
I've got a bunch of
issues assigned to me,

00:44:28.190 --> 00:44:30.023
which is how I'm going
to keep track of what

00:44:30.023 --> 00:44:32.260
stuff I'm supposed to show you.

00:44:32.260 --> 00:44:36.807
So we're going to start by
showing off the logs explorer.

00:44:36.807 --> 00:44:38.640
Some of you have probably
already seen this.

00:44:49.750 --> 00:44:52.260
So this is from the
last time I ran it.

00:44:52.260 --> 00:44:55.200
By default, this will
show you the standard out

00:44:55.200 --> 00:44:59.250
and standard error plus
the Nginx server that's

00:44:59.250 --> 00:45:01.420
in front of your application.

00:45:01.420 --> 00:45:06.810
But you can also pull
in things like VM events

00:45:06.810 --> 00:45:09.550
or the SIS log from the VM.

00:45:09.550 --> 00:45:13.800
And let's get the
whole pile of stuff.

00:45:19.040 --> 00:45:23.510
You can see that we just have
a new audit log event here

00:45:23.510 --> 00:45:28.000
that says that we are starting.

00:45:28.000 --> 00:45:34.120
The operation will tell
us that the operation ID

00:45:34.120 --> 00:45:37.540
and then in the
proto payload, it

00:45:37.540 --> 00:45:39.580
will tell you this
is an audit log

00:45:39.580 --> 00:45:50.935
and that this is
called by service data.

00:45:50.935 --> 00:45:53.930
It was down off the
front of the screen.

00:45:53.930 --> 00:45:55.570
This was a Create
Version request.

00:45:55.570 --> 00:45:59.440
And here is all the
information about the versions.

00:45:59.440 --> 00:46:00.500
You can see it's Flex.

00:46:00.500 --> 00:46:04.300
It's running on VM, manual
scaling, and some other stuff

00:46:04.300 --> 00:46:05.150
that we set.

00:46:05.150 --> 00:46:06.483
Here's our environment variable.

00:46:10.920 --> 00:46:12.210
So let's go back.

00:46:18.980 --> 00:46:19.640
OK.

00:46:19.640 --> 00:46:23.649
So we've talked a little
bit about the logs explorer.

00:46:23.649 --> 00:46:25.190
So next thing I'm
going to talk about

00:46:25.190 --> 00:46:29.670
is how to add log
metrics for data.

00:46:29.670 --> 00:46:36.050
So we've got this re-indexing
piece that I'm doing.

00:46:36.050 --> 00:46:40.530
Let's take a look and
see how we can actually

00:46:40.530 --> 00:46:42.600
keep track of how often
we're calling re-index,

00:46:42.600 --> 00:46:45.420
and is that working the
way we would expect.

00:46:45.420 --> 00:46:48.800
So this is the log based
metric section of Stackdriver.

00:46:48.800 --> 00:46:52.730
You can see it's right
next to the log viewer.

00:46:52.730 --> 00:46:56.750
And in both cases, you can
use this Create Metric link

00:46:56.750 --> 00:46:59.810
to create a new
metric that matches

00:46:59.810 --> 00:47:02.771
a pattern on your application.

00:47:02.771 --> 00:47:04.520
And this is the currently
running version.

00:47:04.520 --> 00:47:09.076
And you can see
there's a lot of--

00:47:09.076 --> 00:47:10.950
well, here's a kernel
message from the kernel

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:11.930
that's booting up.

00:47:11.930 --> 00:47:13.580
But there's also a
lot of health checks

00:47:13.580 --> 00:47:19.290
and some Docker information
and stuff like that.

00:47:19.290 --> 00:47:21.920
So I am actually going
to show you the--

00:47:21.920 --> 00:47:26.280
I've already
created this metric.

00:47:26.280 --> 00:47:28.760
And you can see, if
we go in and edit it,

00:47:28.760 --> 00:47:34.520
that it's actually a query
against the logs API.

00:47:34.520 --> 00:47:36.410
You can use this
same type of query

00:47:36.410 --> 00:47:40.490
to export stuff to
BigQuery or to Pub/Sub.

00:47:40.490 --> 00:47:43.640
And you can see that here
we're looking at standard out

00:47:43.640 --> 00:47:45.760
and standard error
message that says,

00:47:45.760 --> 00:47:48.260
re-indexing is 100% complete.

00:47:48.260 --> 00:47:51.800
And we have a bunch of
these messages from 9:00 PM

00:47:51.800 --> 00:47:53.714
and from last night when
I was working on it.

00:47:58.920 --> 00:48:01.780
So that's how you
set up these metrics.

00:48:01.780 --> 00:48:05.530
Let's take a look at what you
can do with them after you've

00:48:05.530 --> 00:48:06.742
set them up.

00:48:14.530 --> 00:48:17.709
I've added them to the
Stackdriver dashboard.

00:48:22.500 --> 00:48:24.610
There's no data over
the last one hour.

00:48:24.610 --> 00:48:26.250
But if we look
over the last day,

00:48:26.250 --> 00:48:29.550
we can see that I
started some VMs.

00:48:29.550 --> 00:48:32.550
And they did re-indexes
at that time.

00:48:32.550 --> 00:48:34.770
You can also see
the request rate

00:48:34.770 --> 00:48:37.350
and that I was messing around
a bunch with the GR instance

00:48:37.350 --> 00:48:39.750
earlier today and
that at one point,

00:48:39.750 --> 00:48:41.250
the latency was really high.

00:48:41.250 --> 00:48:43.710
But all the rest of
the time, it was low.

00:48:43.710 --> 00:48:45.330
And here's our CPU usage.

00:48:45.330 --> 00:48:47.680
And it looks like we're
really not using two CPUs.

00:48:47.680 --> 00:48:50.820
And probably one would
have been sufficient.

00:48:54.820 --> 00:48:59.490
So we figured out a little bit
about how to use log metrics.

00:48:59.490 --> 00:49:05.440
And now I'm going to talk about
the two different interfaces

00:49:05.440 --> 00:49:09.490
that you've got for looking
at how your app works.

00:49:09.490 --> 00:49:13.935
So one of them is the
standard Cloud Console.

00:49:27.027 --> 00:49:29.110
So one of them is the
standard Cloud Console where

00:49:29.110 --> 00:49:30.950
you can see the basic graphs.

00:49:30.950 --> 00:49:33.400
And there is a
pull-down where you

00:49:33.400 --> 00:49:37.180
can see information for a
particular version and then

00:49:37.180 --> 00:49:42.590
things like CPU utilization
and latency and so forth.

00:49:42.590 --> 00:49:46.270
The other option is to build a
Stackdriver custom dashboard.

00:49:46.270 --> 00:49:47.860
The advantage of the
custom dashboards

00:49:47.860 --> 00:49:50.710
is that you can put
together lots of your data

00:49:50.710 --> 00:49:51.580
into one place.

00:49:51.580 --> 00:49:53.500
And you can put together
different dashboards

00:49:53.500 --> 00:49:54.760
for specific issues.

00:49:54.760 --> 00:49:58.450
So maybe sometimes you
have CPU usage go crazy.

00:49:58.450 --> 00:50:01.540
Maybe you're exporting the
number of backend database

00:50:01.540 --> 00:50:03.610
connections, and that goes wild.

00:50:03.610 --> 00:50:05.370
You can have
different dashboards

00:50:05.370 --> 00:50:06.328
for different purposes.

00:50:13.300 --> 00:50:16.370
So let's see how that
deployment is going.

00:50:16.370 --> 00:50:19.790
So the deployment is finished.

00:50:19.790 --> 00:50:21.270
I'm going to go
back over to Jira.

00:50:21.270 --> 00:50:29.770
And you can see that right
now we are running 731.

00:50:29.770 --> 00:50:39.310
So if we go over to App Engine
and look at the versions that

00:50:39.310 --> 00:50:44.300
are available, we have 732.

00:50:44.300 --> 00:50:45.550
We have one called Base.

00:50:45.550 --> 00:50:47.240
And let's not use
this one called Bad.

00:50:52.046 --> 00:50:52.920
This is an old habit.

00:50:52.920 --> 00:50:55.480
The Migrate Traffic button
is actually a better choice.

00:50:55.480 --> 00:50:57.390
But I'm going to send
100% of the traffic

00:50:57.390 --> 00:50:59.230
to the new instance.

00:50:59.230 --> 00:51:02.340
If this was a public site, I'd
probably start at 5% or 10%.

00:51:02.340 --> 00:51:05.040
This is an internal
tool, so I'm just

00:51:05.040 --> 00:51:08.100
going to swing all the
traffic over to the new site.

00:51:08.100 --> 00:51:13.285
And now let's see
what happens if we--

00:51:13.285 --> 00:51:15.035
I don't know how much
of this gets cached.

00:51:15.035 --> 00:51:16.340
So we're going to
reload the page.

00:51:16.340 --> 00:51:18.589
And it's going to ask me to
log in again, because it's

00:51:18.589 --> 00:51:25.740
a new Jira instance, and it
has a different cookie cache.

00:51:25.740 --> 00:51:27.590
And the LastPass is
not going to help me.

00:51:38.060 --> 00:51:41.760
And so you can see we're back
on the same page we were before.

00:51:41.760 --> 00:51:49.570
But now we are running 732.

00:51:49.570 --> 00:51:53.610
So that's the end of my demo.

00:51:53.610 --> 00:51:56.710
And when you're ready, you
can switch back to the other.

00:51:59.930 --> 00:52:01.040
That's the end of my talk.

00:52:01.040 --> 00:52:04.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

